      "output": "Followup review complete. Report at `runs/mw_protocol_v3_v1_1_application_review_20260913/owner_followup_01/report.md`, probes in `owner_followup_01/reviewer_scratch/`.\n\n**Verdict:** all four prior findings are resolved or correctly dispositioned, the new adjacent changes survived independent challenge — 39/39 of my scratch checks pass — and one new low-severity edge was found (N1). No product acceptance claimed; Trellis untouched.\n\n**Prior findings:**\n- **D1 fixed** — `current()` now maps `graph_plan_binding_mismatch` alongside `graph_run_unknown` to the 404 envelope; probe F1 confirms a crafted GET on a real correction-run id returns 404 with the Chinese message.\n- **R1 fixed end-to-end** — `/recover` (read-only lookup by request identity, works after source supersession), `POST /{id}/resume` gated by a fresh `can_resume` read, and the workspace persisting `pendingRequest` to localStorage before the POST with reload-time recover→resume-once. Probes F2/F3/W1/W2 cover lost-ACK, queued-run resume, and the restart-deadlock I previously flagged is closed.\n- **R2 withdrawn on evidence** — the owner is right: `SqliteUnitOfWork.__enter__` takes `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, so the whole `adopt` critical section including the artifact-manifest write is serialized by the DB write lock across independent store instances and processes. My 4-thread/4-independent-store probe kept all revisions (exactly 1–4) with strict event sequences and full read-back. No extra locks needed; my original concern misread the transaction scope.\n- **D2/R3 accepted as dispositioned** — the workspace's synchronous `submittingRef` guard blocks double-click (W3: exactly one POST); no executor was invented, and I verified dispatches hold only the flock (never the DB lock) across the model call, so there's no concrete correctness issue — only the unmeasured capacity note.\n\n**New changes scrutinized:** lazy product factory behind the default-off mount flag; credentials resolved exactly once at dispatch with the restored probe reused (F8); omp resolver verified against synthetic DBs only (read-only, fail-closed, no fallback substitution); logical identity (brief + sorted source ids) pins the first compile across compiler changes with zero extra calls (F5); legacy graph ids resume and are reused (F4); OS-flock liveness verified with a real cross-process SIGKILL — live dispatch reads `running`/`can_resume:false`, dead owner reads `blocked`, and no path (including direct resume) ever redispatches an UNKNOWN or dead RUNNING shell (F9, F3d/e); frontend staged-files-prepare prohibition, untruncated 600-char brief, and 409-refresh preserving brief/exclusions all confirmed (W4–W6).\n\n**New findings:** N1 (Low) — two simultaneous same-identity POSTs straddling a compiler change can hit the unmapped `graph_root_inputs_conflict` → raw 500; self-heals on retry; minimal fix is catching it in the start route and returning the existing pinned state. N2/N3 informational — dead-dispatch shells have no HTTP reconciliation surface yet, and the liveness probe creates its lock file on read paths (deliberate, benign).\n\nAll 25 manifest hashes re-verified after every probe run — the frozen candidate and live tree are byte-identical to the manifest; nothing outside the followup scratch was written.",
